{"id":"AK-siting_authority-1","technology":"solar;wind","policy_type":"siting_authority","who_decides":"local_government","state_body":null,"threshold_mw":null,"summary":"Boroughs and cities with planning powers regulate land use, including permits that encourage or discourage specified uses; no state body issues a siting permit for wind or solar. A wind or solar plant that sells power to the public needs a certificate of public convenience and necessity from the Regulatory Commission of Alaska, but a renewable plant of up to 65 MW is exempt if it first entered commercial operation on or after August 31, 2010 and before July 1, 2028, sells only to commission-regulated utilities, and its owner or operator received no state grant used to generate the power and no state tax credit for that generation.","statute_citation":"Alaska Stat. 29.40.040; 42.05.221(a); 42.05.990(6); 42.05.711(r)","statute_url":"https://www.akleg.gov/basis/statutes.asp?media=print&secStart=29.40.040&secEnd=29.40.040","evidence_level":"primary_source","verification":"verified","reviewed_on":"2026-10-09"}
